{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791728037,"nonce":"YJDR054PflUiFePkG82piqBrCIU=","title":"Canvas 3D: meshes, materials, lights and glTF","message":"The canvas element has GPU commands and a 2D context is queued (11ae770), but no 3D. The other SDK draws 3D on its canvas: meshes with PBR materials (a row of spheres from polished to rough, plastic above and metal below), a key light and point lights, a camera, textures, glTF models with poses, and 2D drawn over them. Its scenes are CanvasMesh and GltfPosed under tools/snapshot/scenes; its types are ashui.draw3d (Camera, Light, Material, MeshData), ashui.math (Vec3, Quat, Mat4), and the renderer's MeshShader, MeshTextures and ScenePainter.\n\nBlinc itself has a 3D pipeline (blinc_gpu's mesh pipeline: lighting, bloom, depth), so this should reuse the engine's, as the other SDK does, not a second one in TypeScript.\n\nScope:\n- A 3D drawing for the canvas element, beside the existing GPU drawing: a depth buffer per canvas, a camera, lights (directional, point), meshes with a transform and a material.\n- Materials: base colour and texture, metallic and roughness, normal and emissive maps as the engine allows.\n- glTF loading, with node transforms and a pose, then skins and animation later.\n- Mixing with 2D: the canvas's own 2D drawing over the 3D, and the 3D clipped and layered like any canvas (it is in paint order, under glass and layers).\n- TypeScript API in the style of the canvas commands: typed Camera, Light, Material, Mesh, with math helpers (Vec3, Quat, Mat4).\n- Memory: depth and any HDR targets sized to the canvas box and freed when it is destroyed, with the renderer's idle release; the wiki's performance panel shows them.\n\nLearn from the other SDK first: read how it structures the scene passes (ScenePainter, ScenePassFrame) and its mesh shader before designing, and compare with blinc_gpu's mesh pipeline.\n\nTests: a lit sphere row compared by pixels at 1x and 2x, a glTF model posed, a canvas under a glass layer, destroyed and recreated without growth.\nWiki: a 3D section on the GPU canvas page with live examples and their source, matching the 2D one.\n\nRelated: 11ae770 (Canvas 2D context).","files":null}]}